2. Impaired neurotransmitter production and release: Neurotransmitters are chemicals that help regulate the transmission of signals between neurons. Neurodegenerative diseases can lead to a decrease in the production and release of neurotransmitters, such as dopamine, dopamine, and serotonin, which can impair the normal functioning of the nervous system.

3. Abnormal protein aggregation: In some neurodegenerative diseases, such as Alzheimer's disease and sickle cell disease, abnormal proteins begin to aggregate in the blood and block the normal functioning of neurons. These aggregates can block the growth of new neurons and prevent the normal transmission of signals between existing neurons.

