6. Loss of stem cell function: Stem cells are responsible for the production of new neurons and the repair of damaged neurons. Neurodegenerative diseases can lead to a decrease in the function of stem cells, which can contribute to the loss of neurons and the disruption of the normal functioning of the nervous system.

In summary, neurodegenerative diseases affect the transmission of signals between neurons in the brain through various mechanisms, including damage to the myelin sheath, impaired neurotransmitter production and release, abnormal protein aggregation, impaired cell signaling pathways, oxidative stress and inflammation, and loss of stem cell function. These mechanisms collectively lead to the progressive loss of neurons and the disruption of the normal functioning of the nervous system.

