","To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we can use the following equation:

ΔS = ΣnS(products) - ΣnS(reactants)

where n is the number of moles of each substance and S is the standard entropy of the substance.

For the reaction:

3 CO(g) + O2(g) → 3 CO2(g)

The standard entropies (S°) at 298 K are:

S°(CO) = 197.3 J/mol·K
S°(O2) = 205.0 J/mol·K
S°(CO2) = 213.8 J/mol·K

Now, we can plug these values into the equation:

ΔS = [3 × S°(CO2)] - [3 × S°(CO) + S°(O2)]

ΔS = [3 × 213.8 J/mol·K] - [3 × 197.3 J/mol·K + 205.0 J/mol·K]

ΔS = [641.4 J/mol·K] - [591.9 J/mol·K + 205.0 J/mol·K]

ΔS = [641.4 J/mol·K] - [796.9 J/mol·K]

ΔS = -155.5 J/mol·K

The change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction is -155.5 J/mol·K.
