","Absolute asymmetric synthesis is a type of asymmetric synthesis that allows the production of a single enantiomer (a single-handed version of a molecule) with 100% enantiomeric excess (ee). This means that the reaction produces only the desired enantiomer, with no contamination from the other enantiomer. This is the highest level of enantioselectivity achievable in asymmetric synthesis.

The difference between absolute asymmetric synthesis and other types of asymmetric synthesis lies in the way the reaction is controlled to favor one enantiomer over the other. In other types of asymmetric synthesis, such as enantioselective synthesis, the reaction may produce a high ee of the desired enantiomer, but it may not be 100%. In these cases, the reaction may still produce a small amount of the undesired enantiomer, which can be difficult to remove in some cases.

In contrast, in absolute asymmetric synthesis, the reaction is designed to produce only the desired enantiomer, with no contamination from the other enantiomer. This is typically achieved by using chiral catalysts or reagents that selectively interact with one enantiomer over the other, leading to a complete enantioselective reaction.

An example of absolute asymmetric synthesis is the synthesis of the chiral auxiliary 1-phenylpyrrolidnylamine, which was achieved by the Meerwein-Ponndorf-Veriegler (MPV) reaction using a chiral catalyst. The reaction produced the desired enantiomer with 100% ee, demonstrating the ability of asymmetric synthesis to produce enantiomerically pure compounds.

In summary, absolute asymmetric synthesis is the most efficient and selective form of asymmetric synthesis, providing 100% enantiomerically pure products. This is achieved by using chiral catalysts or reagents that selectively interact with one enantiomer, leading to a complete enantioselective reaction.
