","Isopopologues and isotopomers are two terms used to describe variations in molecules based on isotopic substitutions. The key difference lies in whether the substitution affects the molecular structure or just the mass distribution. 

1. **Isopologues**: Isopologues are molecules that have the same molecular formula but differ in the isotopic composition of their atoms. For example, \( \text{C}_2\text{H}_2 \) and \( \text{C}_2\text{D}_2 \) are isotopologues because they have the same molecular formula but differ in the presence of deuterium (D) instead of hydrogen (H). Isopologues are chemically similar but may exhibit different physical properties, such as chemical shift in NMR spectroscopy or infrared absorption frequencies.

2. **Isotopomers**: Isotopomers are molecules that have the same molecular structure but differ in the isotopic composition of their atoms. For example, \( \text{C}_2\text{H}_2 \) and \( \text{C}_2\text{H}^1\text{D} \) are isotopomers because they have the same molecular structure but differ in the presence of one deuterium atom (D) instead of hydrogen (H). Isotopomers are chemically identical but may have different physical properties, such as mass or rotational constants.

In summary, the key difference is that isotopologues can have different molecular structures (e.g., \( \text{C}_2\text{H}_2 \) and \( \text{C}_2\text{D}_2 \)), while isotopomers have the same molecular structure (e.g., \( \text{C}_2\text{H}_2 \) and \( \text{C}_2\text{H}^1\text{D} \)). Both terms refer to variations in isotopic composition, but the term ""isopologues"" is more used in the context of chemical differences, while ""isotomers"" is more used in the context of physical differences.
