Calculate the activation energy \( E_a \) for the reaction \( H_2 + I_2 \rightarrow 2HI \) using the Arrhenius equation. Given the forward rate constants \( k_{f1} = 10^{-1} \) at \( 1/T_1 = 0.0018 \, \text{K}^{-1} \) and \( k_{f2} = 10^4 \) at \( 1/T_2 = 0.0012 \, \text{K}^{-1} \), and the gas constant \( R = 2 \, \text{cal K}^{-1} \text{mol}^{-1} \).
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the activation energy \( E_a \), we use the Arrhenius equation:

\[
k = A e^{-E_a / RT}
\]

Taking the natural logarithm of both sides:

\[
\ln k = \ln A - \frac{E_a}{RT}
\]

For two sets of data (\( k_{f1} \), \( T_1 \) and \( k_{f2} \), \( T_2 \)), we have:

\[
\ln k_{f1} = \ln A - \frac{E_a}{RT_1}
\]

\[
\ln k_{f2} = \ln A - \frac{E_a}{RT_2}
\]

Subtracting the first equation from the second:

\[
\ln k_{f2} - \ln k_{f1} = \frac{E_a}{R} \left( \frac{1}{T_1} - \frac{1}{T_2} \right)
\]

Solving for \( E_a \):

\[
E_a = \frac{\ln k_{f2} - \ln k_{f1}}{\frac{1}{T_1} - \frac{1}{T_2}} \cdot R
\]

Substituting the given values:

\[
E_a = \frac{\ln (10^4) - \ln (10^{-1})}{\frac{1}{277.7 \, \text{K}} - \frac{1}{1000 \, \text{K}}} \cdot 2 \, \text{cal K}^{-1} \text{mol}^{-1}
\]

\[
E_a = \frac{4 + 1}{0.0039 - 0.001} \, \text{cal K}^{-1} \text{mol}^{-1}
\]

\[
E_a = \frac{5}{0.0029} \, \text{cal K}^{-1} \text{mol}^{-1}
\]

\[
E_a = \frac{5}{0.0029} \, \text{cal K}^{-1} \text{mol}^{-1} \approx 1703 \, \text{cal mol}^{-1}
\]

Thus, the activation energy is \(\boxed{1703 \, \text{cal mol}^{-1}}\).

