","The net chemical reaction for the discharging of a lead storage battery is:
\[ 2\text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow \text{H}_2 + \text{O}_2 + 2\text{H}^+ + 2\text{e}^- \]
This reaction occurs at the anode and cathode of the battery, with the hydrogen gas being produced at the anode and the oxygen gas being produced at the cathode. The overall reaction can be written as:
\[ \text{Pb} + \text{PbO}_2 + 2\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4 \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4 + \text{PbSO}_4 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} \]
This reaction is not balanced, as the number of electrons and protons are not equal on both sides. The balanced reaction is:
\[ \text{Pb} + \text{PbO}_2 + 2\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4 \rightarrow 2\text{PbSO}_4 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} \]
This reaction is the overall reaction that occurs during the discharging of a lead storage battery. The lead and lead dioxide electrodes are reduced and oxidized, respectively, and the sulfate ions from the sulfuric acid solution react with the lead and lead dioxide to form lead sulfate. The overall reaction is exothermic, and the energy released is used to power the electrical circuit.
