(v) The volumetric area ratio up to which the lumpuature model can be used.
model
(i) **Temperature after 5 minutes**:  
We use the lumped-parameter method for this, assuming the temperature is uniform within the component. The temperature \( T(t) \) is given by:  
\[
T(t) = T_0 + (T_i - T_0) e^{-hA t / \rho V c_p}
\]  
where:  
- \( T_0 = 20^\circ \text{C} \) (ambient temperature),  
- \( T_i = 345^\circ \text{C} \) (initial temperature),  
- \( h = 56.8 \, \text{W/m}^2 \text{K} \) (convective heat transfer coefficient),  
- \( A = 0.075 \, \text{m}^2 \) (surface area),  
- \( \rho = 7150 \, \text{kg/m}^3 \) (density),  
- \( V = \frac{1.2}{7150} = 1.63 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{m}^3 \) (volume),  
- \( c_p = 385 \, \text{J/kg K} \) (specific heat),  
- \( t = 5 \, \text{min} = 300 \, \text{s} \).  

Substituting the values:  
\[
T(300) = 20 + (345 - 20) e^{-56.8 \times 0.075 \times 300 / (7150 \times 1.63 \times 10^{-4} \times 385)} = 20 + (325) e^{-0.0111} = 20 + 325 \times 0.9909 = 325.09^\circ \text{C}
\]  

**Final temperature after 5 minutes**: \(\boxed{325.09^\circ \text{C}}\).  

(ii) **Time required to reach 50°C**:  
Rearrange the lumped-parameter equation to solve for \( t \):  
\[
t = \frac{\rho V c_p}{hA} \ln \left( \frac{T_i - T_0}{T(t) - T_0} \right)
\]  
Substitute \( T(t) = 50^\circ \text{C} \):  
\[
t = \frac{7150 \times 1.63 \times 10^{-4} \times 385}{56.8 \times 0.075} \ln \left( \frac{345 - 20}{50 - 20} \right) = 1.01 \times 10^3 \ln(1.15) = 1.0
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

