","The maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a photon scattered by an electron is given by the Compton effect formula:  
\[
\Delta \lambda = \frac{2 \pi \hbar}{m_e c} (1 - \cos \theta)
\]  
where:  
- \(\Delta \lambda\) is the change in wavelength,  
- \(\hbar\) is the reduced Planck constant (\(1.054 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J s}\)),  
- \(m_e\) is the electron mass (\(9.109 \times 10^{-31} \, \text{kg}\)),  
- \(c\) is the speed of light (\(2.998 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}\)),  
- \(\theta\) is the scattering angle.  

For the maximum wavelength change, \(\theta = 180^\circ\), so \(\cos \theta = -1\). Substituting the values:  
\[
\Delta \lambda = \frac{2 \pi \times 1.054 \times 10^{-34}}{9.109 \times 10^{-31} \times 2.998 \times 10^8} \times 2 = 1.22 \times 10^{-10} \, \text{m} = 1.22 \, \text{Å}
\]  

The percentage change is:  
\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{\Delta \lambda}{\lambda_0} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{1.22}{2.00} \right) \times 100 = 61.0\%
\]  

Thus, the maximum percentage change in the wavelength is \(\boxed{61.0\%}\).
