In a Cartesian coordinate system, what is the curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) that depends only on \(x\) and \(y\)? Express the result in terms of its components.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) in a Cartesian coordinate system is given by the divergence operator \(\nabla \times \mathbf{V}\). In Cartesian coordinates, this is expressed as:

\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
\frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \\
V_x & V_y & V_z
\end{vmatrix}
\]

Since \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) depends only on \(x\) and \(y\), \(V_z = 0\). The curl simplifies to:

\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \mathbf{i} \left( \frac{\partial V_z}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial z} \right) - \mathbf{j} \left( \frac{\partial V_z}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial z} \right) + \mathbf{k} \left( \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} \right)
\]

Since \(V_z = 0\), the curl becomes:

\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \mathbf{k} \left( \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} \right)
\]

Thus, the curl of \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) is a vector along the \(z\)-axis, with magnitude equal to the difference of the partial derivatives of \(V_y\) with respect to \(x\) and \(V_x\) with respect to \(y\). 

\[
\boxed{\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}}
\]
