A proton with mass \(1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}\) and charge \(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) accelerates from rest in a uniform electric field of strength \(500 \, \text{N/C}\).  
(a) What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the proton?  
(b) How long does it take the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\)?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","(a) The force on the proton is given by \(F = qE\), where \(q\) is the charge of the proton and \(E\) is the electric field strength. The net force is the only force acting on the proton, so the acceleration \(a\) is given by Newton's second law:  
\[
a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{qE}{m} = \frac{(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C})}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 3.96 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2.
\]  
(b) The time \(t\) the proton takes to reach a speed \(v\) is given by the kinematic equation:  
\[
v = u + at,
\]  
where \(u = 0\) (since the proton starts from rest). Solving for \(t\):  
\[
t = \frac{v - u}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s}}{3.96 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2} = 8.64 \times 10^{-8} \, \text{s}.
\]  
Final answers:  
(a) The magnitude of the acceleration is \(\boxed{3.96 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2}\).  
(b) The time required is \(\boxed{8.64 \times 10^{-8} \, \text{s}}\).
