","To calculate the change in Gibbs free energy (ΔG) for the reaction, we can use the following equation:

ΔG = ΔG(products) - ΔG(reactants)

For the reaction between hydrogen gas (H2) and oxygen gas (O2) to form water (H2O), the balanced equation is:

2H2(g) + O2(g) → 2H2O(l)

The standard Gibbs free energy of formation (ΔGf°) of water is given as -237.1 kJ/mol. Since the standard Gibbs free energy of formation of the elements in their standard state (H2 and O2) is 0 kJ/mol, we can calculate the change in Gibbs free energy for the reaction:

ΔG = [2 × ΔGf°(H2O)] - [2 × ΔGf°(H2) + ΔGf°(O2)]
ΔG = [2 × (-237.1 kJ/mol)] - [2 × 0 kJ/mol + 0 kJ/mol]
ΔG = -474.2 kJ/mol

Since we have 2 moles of hydrogen gas and 1 mole of oxygen gas reacting, we need to multiply the change in Gibbs free energy by the stoichiometric coefficients:

ΔG = -474.2 kJ/mol × 2 = -948.4 kJ

The change in Gibbs free energy for the reaction is -948.4 kJ.
